Print a root-volume file to the console at chosen boot times (sweetdumpfile).

The userland probe runs as a launchd daemon and cannot open /dev/console, so
its output is lost; the probe now writes /private/var/tmp/logdump.out and this
kernel thread prints that file to the console instead.  Nothing changes unless
both boot args are set:

  sweetdumpfile=<absolute path>      e.g. /private/var/tmp/logdump.out
  sweetdumptimes=<secs>[,<secs>...]  e.g. 240,360,600 (boot uptime seconds)

At each configured uptime a kernel thread opens the file read-only
(vnode_open), reads it in 8 KiB chunks (vn_rdwr in a kernel context, 512 KiB
cap) and prints:

  PDUMP <path> err=<e> size=<n> uptime=<s>   (err != 0: file missing/unopenable)
  PDUMP| <line>                              (max 200 chars, max 400 lines)
  PDUMP end <lines> lines

so "no output" is distinguishable from "probe never wrote the file".  Runs in
the same file as 0126's fsprobe and is started from the same place.

Base: bsd/dev/sweet_sample.c from the CI tree after 0126; only that file
changes.
--- a/bsd/dev/sweet_sample.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/sweet_sample.c
